                    'If you&#x27;re emailing someone outside ... your email address, include your name or company. &quot;Meeting Request — [Your Name], [Company]&quot; ensures they know who&#x27;s asking. An anonymous meeting request from an unfamiliar address goes straight to the &quot;ignore&quot; pile. &quot;Meeting ASAP&quot; for a routine discussion undermines your credibility. Save urgency language for genuinely urgent situations. Everything else should suggest a reasonable timeframe — &quot;this week,&quot; &quot;next week,&quot; ...\nIf you\'re emailing someone outside your organization who might not recognize your email address, include your name or company. "Meeting Request — [Your Name], [Company]" ensures they know who\'s asking. An anonymous meeting request from an unfamiliar address goes straight to the "ignore" pile. "Meeting ASAP" for a routine discussion undermines your credibility. Save urgency language for genuinely urgent situations. Everything else should suggest a reasonable timeframe — "this week," "next week," or "before [date]." People who cry wolf with urgency stop getting meetings altogether.\nMeeting request email subject lines that get accepted. 60+ examples for scheduling business meetings, calls, demos, and one-on-ones — with calendar psychology and strategy.\nPro tip: Reserve "urgent" for genuinely time-sensitive requests — customer escalations, deal-closing deadlines, production incidents. If you label every meeting request as urgent, people stop taking your urgency seriously.\nA meeting request lives or dies by its subject line. People protect their calendars more fiercely than their inboxes — every meeting accepted is time taken from deep work, other priorities, or personal life. You need to give them a clear, compelling reason to say yes before they even open your email.',
